 "response": "Volidrox's budget is now **$1,626K**.\n\n**How I worked it out**\n1. Only Finance Office messages count. Vendor, Legal, Engineering, Operations and similar messages do not change the budget. Finance Office notices that aren't budget changes (reminders, receipts, audits, cost-centre codes) don't either.\n2. For each withdrawal, I traced its chain from the latest link backwards. The last withdrawal in a chain is never itself withdrawn, so it is in effect. Each link below it then alternates between in effect and cancelled. Examples:\n   - #3: #146 withdraws #26. That means #26 doesn't count, so #15 stands. #15 withdraws #4, so #4 doesn't count. **#3 applies (\u2212$196K).**\n   - #56: #910 \u2192 #553 \u2192 #222 \u2192 #162 \u2192 #79 \u2192 #69 \u2192 #56. Six links means #69 is cancelled. **#56 applies (\u2212$549K).**\n   - #23: #842 \u2192 #136 \u2192 #30 \u2192 #23. #30 stands, so **#23 is cancelled.**\n3. After resolving every chain, about 120 budget changes are cancelled. Some updates that look cancelled are restored because their withdrawal was itself withdrawn. These include #10, #12, #27, #29, #33, #39, #54, #64, #87, #117, #221, #246, #251, #278, #307, #580, #597, #614, #654, #678 and #1363.\n4. I started from the $2,760K set in #0001 and applied every remaining change in record order, which leaves $1,626K. This is one long hand calculation, so a single slip would change the total.\n\nANSWER: $1626K",
